


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: wojciechowski
@title: Between Scholarship and the Public: The Polish Music Portal as a Digital Resource on Polish Composers
@presenter: wojciechowski
@abstract:

How can we present the music of nineteenth-century Polish
composers without flattening its message, while still using
the means of communication of the twenty-first century? The
Polish Music Portal, developed by the Fryderyk Chopin
Institute, brings together in one place resources that
researchers in Poland and abroad have usually had to gather
from many institutions and publications. When we follow the
trail of a manuscript, a printed edition or a single
performance, the Portal places these within a wider network
of sources, places and stories.

This paper shows how these resources can be combined into a
coherent tool for researching nineteenth-century Polish
music. Its case study is Ignacy Jan Paderewski’s Piano
Concerto in A minor, Op. 17. The starting point is the
record for the work, which links to the sources (e.g.
digitised manuscripts) and to current literature and
bibliography. I then consider how the musical text can be
compared with available recordings, so that performers’
interpretations and their changes over time can be traced.
I also show how the scoring can be examined, and how the
composer’s output can be searched for other works for the
same forces.

Particular attention is given to biographical context. The
chronology of the concerto’s composition and premiere is
set against the composer’s biography and timeline, and
then against his correspondence, which may hold traces of
his work on the piece, his exchanges with performers and
opinions on its reception. Using the map of Paderewski’s
activities, the places where the concerto was written and
performed can be linked to his travels, concerts and work
in various countries. This shows how the composer’s life
and mobility shaped the circulation of the work.

The paper ends by weighing the strengths and limitations of
this approach: data completeness that varies from one
resource to another, the need to verify information
critically, and the need to read the Portal’s resources
alongside the literature and source editions. It proposes a
model that can be applied to other Polish compositions, and
it points to the Portal’s potential for research,
teaching and making musical heritage accessible to the
public.


